[THOUGHT]  
All diagnostic data points converge robustly on **diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL), stage IV** — the most common aggressive B-cell lymphoma in adults. Key evidence includes:  
- Age- and sex-appropriate presentation (55-year-old male);  
- Systemic “B symptoms”: drenching night sweats + >10% weight loss (12 lbs ≈ 10% of average body weight for this age);  
- Physical findings: splenomegaly, left lower quadrant dullness (suggesting possible splenic or peritoneal involvement), abdominal distension/fullness;  
- Imaging: bulky, multi-compartmental lymphadenopathy (cervical, axillary, mediastinal) — consistent with widespread nodal disease;  
- Histopathology: CD20+ lymphocytes with high proliferative index — classic immunophenotype of DLBCL;  
- Absence of contradictory features (e.g., no t(11;14) or cyclin D1 expression to suggest mantle cell; no follicular architecture or BCL2 translocation to support follicular lymphoma);  
- Bone marrow biopsy not performed — but *not required* for diagnosis of DLBCL when extranodal or advanced nodal disease is confirmed; staging can proceed clinically and radiologically (e.g., PET-CT), and treatment initiation need not await marrow results in symptomatic, high-burden disease.

[SUMMARY]  
**Diagnosis:** Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL), Ann Arbor Stage IV (involving lymph nodes above and below the diaphragm — cervical, axillary, mediastinal — plus splenic and probable abdominal/extranodal involvement).  
**Prognosis:** Intermediate-to-high risk by IPI (International Prognostic Index): age ≥60? No (55), but elevated LDH (not yet measured), ECOG ≥2 (fatigue, weight loss, night sweats likely indicate ≥1), >1 extranodal site (spleen + abdomen), and bulky disease — likely IPI score 2–3.  
**Next Immediate Steps (per NCCN/ESMO guidelines):**  
- Staging PET-CT (if not already completed with CT thorax/abdomen);  
- Baseline labs: CBC, CMP, LDH, β2-microglobulin, HIV/HBV/HCV serologies;  
- Initiation of R-CHOP (rituximab, cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone) as first-line therapy — standard of care for fit patients with DLBCL;  
- Referral to hematologic oncology for treatment planning and supportive care (e.g., growth factor support, infection prophylaxis, nutritional assessment).  

No further diagnostic questioning is indicated. Clinical diagnosis is complete and actionable.

